## RabbitMQ Guide

`make docker-up` starts RabbitMQ from `docker-compose.yml`, and `make docker-down` stops it. Its management UI is at `http://localhost:15672`; sign in with `RABBITMQ_USER` and `RABBITMQ_PASSWORD` from `.env`.

`make run` publishes one sample `user.created` event to the durable queue `{{.Slug}}-users` (`RABBITMQ_QUEUE`), then consumes the queue until Ctrl+C. {{if .Database}}Each `user.created` event becomes a user in {{.Database}}, once per event id, so each run adds exactly one user.{{else}}It logs each event's type, id and size.{{end}} A message is acknowledged only after it is processed, so one in progress when the worker stops goes back to the queue. The "Broker workflow" section of `AGENTS.md` describes the event format and the delivery guarantees.

### Send an event

The worker reads event envelopes; anything else is logged and dropped. The `id` must be a new UUID for each event. Publish from the management UI (Queues → `{{.Slug}}-users` → Publish message), or from the container:

```text
$ docker compose exec rabbitmq rabbitmqadmin --username <RABBITMQ_USER> --password <RABBITMQ_PASSWORD> \
    publish message --routing-key {{.Slug}}-users \
    --payload '{"id":"7c1f8f9e-8a8e-4a43-9a43-0f9f4b8c2f10","type":"user.created","version":1,"data":{"name":"Ada"}}'
```

The worker logs `{{if .Database}}event persisted{{else}}event received{{end}}` with the event's type and id.

### Publisher-only and subscriber-only workers

Delete the `broker.Consume(...)` call in `cmd/main.go` for a pure publisher, or the sample `Publish(...)` for a pure subscriber.
